// @corpus: platform
// @id: PLATFORM-log
// @category: platform — logging
// @charter: §7.8, §10
// @milestone: E2C
//
// Logging is an effect PARAMETERIZED BY PRIVACY LEVEL, which is the whole
// mechanism: `log<Public>` names a sink whose contents may be read by anyone,
// so a `Secret<Payments>` reaching it (corpus R-006) is a flow violation the
// compiler can see rather than an incident someone reports later. The level
// lives in the row and not in the function's name, so a checker reads it from
// the signature instead of recognising `public`.

module log

import capability.{ Private, Public }

// **A sink records any value** and polices its privacy label, which is read
// from the effect row's type argument. Declared over `String` until
// 2026-09-24, so logging a `Secret<Payments>` was a type error as well as the
// privacy violation the sink exists to refuse — two verdicts about one
// defect, and the type relation reaching it first.
fn public<T>(message: T) -> () !{ log<Public> } { () }

fn private<T>(message: T) -> () !{ log<Private> } { () }
